  "approved_angle": {
    "title": "I want to know what made a couple book, as well as what they loved afterward",
    "core_perspective": "I want to understand what a couple was weighing when they chose your business. Their review can tell us what they loved about the experience. Asking about the booking decision can give us different information to consider when we write your ads."
  },
  "sharpened_point": null,
  "strategic_purpose": {
    "pillar": "ATTRACT",
    "content_type": "founder_forward",
    "why_now": "Show prospective clients the questions behind WIA's advertising decisions. This develops the existing approved research angle into a format set, rather than creating another campaign about positioning or campaign settings. Its value is the distinction between understanding the experience couples loved and understanding the decision they made."
  },
  "audience": {
    "who": "Wedding business owners with strong reviews who already invest in marketing and want their advertising to reflect why their best-fit couples chose them.",
    "reason_to_care": "They may reasonably think their reviews give an ads team everything it needs to understand their appeal. This piece respects that evidence while introducing another useful question: what mattered to the couple while they were still choosing?"
  },
  "intended_shift": {
    "understand": "A review and an account of the booking decision can answer different questions, and both can be useful when considering advertising messages.",
    "believe": "WIA's advertising thinking includes understanding the couple's decision, alongside the experience the business delivers.",
    "do": "Consider what they know about why their best-fit couples booked, alongside what those couples praised afterward."
  },
  "point_of_view": "I",
  "wia_perspective": "I was talking with our team about how we could learn more from people who choose us, and it made me think about the information we ask wedding businesses to share. When we onboard an ELEVATE client, we ask whether they have market research we can look at. Their reviews are useful, and I also want to understand what was happening before the wedding: what the couple was weighing and what helped them choose. I proposed asking newly booked couples about that decision, with their answers becoming another source to consider for ad copy and landing pages. That was an idea to explore. The perspective worth sharing is that understanding the decision gives us more to work with when we decide what to communicate in an ad.",
  "supporting_example": {
    "scene": "This was an internal conversation about improving WIA's own marketing, not a client interview. Whitney described asking new ELEVATE clients whether they had market research to share and contrasted that question with the reviews clients offered.",
    "wia_move": "Whitney proposed asking newly signed clients about their buying decision and considering the resulting information in ad copy and landing pages. A colleague agreed with the idea. The supplied discussion does not establish that the workflow was implemented or that answers were collected.",
    "client_contribution": "No client speaks in this scene. There are no recorded couple responses, client-approved figures or client outcomes to use. The example demonstrates Whitney's question and proposal, not a finding about why any particular couple booked."
  },
  "narrative_outline": {
    "hooks": [
      {
        "style": "observation",
        "text": "I'm interested in the moment a couple decided to choose you, as well as the experience they describe afterward."
      },
      {
        "style": "question",
        "text": "What did your best-fit couple need to know before they felt ready to book?"
      },
      {
        "style": "contrarian_belief",
        "text": "A glowing review can leave a useful advertising question open: what helped this couple choose you?"
      }
    ],
    "problems_desires_symptoms": [
      "Owners have thoughtful reviews that describe what couples appreciated about their wedding experience.",
      "They want ads that communicate the reasons their business is a good fit for the couples they want to reach.",
      "The question is whether those reviews also explain what mattered during the booking decision."
    ],
    "main_point": "Ask about the choice as well as the experience. What a couple was weighing before booking can give WIA different information to consider when writing ads.",
    "transformation": "The owner starts looking at their evidence through two useful lenses: what their business delivered and what helped a couple decide. WIA gains another possible source for messaging decisions, without claiming that answers have been collected or performance has changed.",
    "cta": "No explicit ask. Close on the value of understanding the couple's decision, without directing readers toward an offer."
  },
  "format_plan": {
    "reels": [
      {
        "job": "Introduce the distinction through Whitney's own thinking. Use one newly recorded script, since the historical footage includes broad claims and an unimplemented workflow that should not become the public message.",
        "cover_text": "What mattered before they booked?",
        "spoken_opening": "What would you ask a couple right after they chose you for their wedding?",
        "beats": [
          "When we were discussing research for our own marketing, I raised a question about the information we ask our wedding business clients to share.",
          "Their reviews can tell us what couples loved about the experience. I also want to know what those couples were weighing when they decided to book.",
          "What were they comparing? What did they need to understand? What helped them feel ready to choose? Those are questions I'd want to explore, rather than answers I'd assume.",
          "I proposed asking newly booked couples about that decision and considering what we learn when we write ad copy and landing pages.",
          "That remains an idea to explore. But understanding why someone chose you can give us something different to work with, alongside what they loved afterward."
        ],
        "read_time_seconds": 65,
        "source": "new_script",
        "evidence_ids": [
          "ev_fd7910749f6b4ea23a88cb58",
          "ev_63129cd508558d77a01ff68b",
          "ev_98fb46a1eff79bc1765f98e8",
          "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327",
          "ev_cc67e45e49c59eb19d6fc178",
          "ev_4cf6a239636595d938c9fc24",
          "ev_da4c80edaa2541df2a5af54b"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "carousel": {
      "job": "Make the two kinds of evidence easy to distinguish, then show how Whitney's proposed question connects to advertising decisions. Keep it evergreen and free of offer mechanics.",
      "slides": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "job": "Open with the distinction.",
          "text": "Your reviews are one part of the story behind a booking. They can describe the experience a couple loved. There's another part I'm interested in when we think about advertising: what mattered while that couple was deciding whether to choose you.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_98fb46a1eff79bc1765f98e8",
            "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "job": "Respect the evidence owners already have.",
          "text": "A thoughtful review gives us something useful to read. It can show which parts of the wedding experience a couple appreciated enough to describe afterward. I'd want to consider that language when thinking about how to communicate your business.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_98fb46a1eff79bc1765f98e8",
            "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "job": "Introduce questions about the earlier decision.",
          "text": "I'd also want to ask about the time before they booked. What were they weighing? What did they need to understand about your business, and what helped them feel ready to choose? These are questions to explore with couples, rather than reasons to supply on their behalf.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_63129cd508558d77a01ff68b",
            "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 4,
          "job": "Ground the idea in Whitney's actual contribution.",
          "text": "During an internal conversation about our own marketing, I described a question we ask new ELEVATE clients: do they have market research to share with us? That conversation made room for looking at the booking decision alongside the reviews a business already has.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_fd7910749f6b4ea23a88cb58",
            "ev_63129cd508558d77a01ff68b",
            "ev_98fb46a1eff79bc1765f98e8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 5,
          "job": "Describe the proposal at its actual status.",
          "text": "I proposed asking newly booked couples about their decision. Their answers could become another source to consider when we write ad copy and landing pages. It was an idea to explore, rather than a completed research project with findings to share.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_cc67e45e49c59eb19d6fc178",
            "ev_4cf6a239636595d938c9fc24",
            "ev_da4c80edaa2541df2a5af54b"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 6,
          "job": "Explain how an answer could inform the work.",
          "text": "The useful next question would be what an answer helps us communicate. Does it point to something a couple needed explained, or something they wanted to feel confident about? We'd consider that information when deciding what to say, without assuming every couple chooses for the same reason.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327",
            "ev_4cf6a239636595d938c9fc24",
            "ev_da4c80edaa2541df2a5af54b"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 7,
          "job": "Land the principle without an invitation.",
          "text": "Understanding what couples loved afterward helps us understand the experience you deliver. Understanding what mattered beforehand can help us think about the choice you're asking another couple to make. Both deserve consideration when we communicate your business through advertising.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_98fb46a1eff79bc1765f98e8",
            "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327",
            "ev_da4c80edaa2541df2a5af54b"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "email": {
      "subject_options": [
        "What helped your couples choose you?",
        "The decision behind a great review",
        "What I'd ask a newly booked couple"
      ],
      "preview": "Their booking decision can give us something different to consider when we write ads.",
      "opening": "Hi {{contact.first_name}},\n\nI've been thinking about the questions we ask couples after they book. Their reviews can tell us what they loved about the experience. I'm also interested in what mattered while they were still deciding.",
      "argument": "During an internal conversation about improving our own marketing, I described how we ask new ELEVATE clients whether they have market research to share.\n\nReviews are useful evidence to consider. Alongside them, I'd want to understand what a couple was weighing before choosing that business. What did they need to know? What helped them feel ready to book?\n\nI proposed asking newly signed clients about that decision and considering their answers in ad copy and landing pages. That was an idea to explore, rather than a completed research project.",
      "transformation": "The distinction gives us another way to think about the information behind an ad. What a couple appreciated afterward tells us about the experience. What helped them choose can give us different information about the decision another couple may be considering.",
      "ending": "That's the part I'm interested in understanding: the choice that came before the review, alongside the experience that followed it.\n\nTalk soon,\nWhitney",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev_fd7910749f6b4ea23a88cb58",
        "ev_63129cd508558d77a01ff68b",
        "ev_98fb46a1eff79bc1765f98e8",
        "ev_2a47c48f852edd53262d1327",
        "ev_cc67e45e49c59eb19d6fc178",
        "ev_4cf6a239636595d938c9fc24",
        "ev_da4c80edaa2541df2a5af54b"
      ]
    },
    "caption": {
      "hook": "The moment a couple chooses you is worth understanding in its own right.",
      "adds": "You may already have thoughtful reviews that describe what couples loved about working with you. Those are useful to consider when we think about your advertising.\n\nI also want to understand what happened before they booked. What were they weighing, and what helped them feel ready to choose your business?\n\nIn an internal conversation about our own marketing, I described how we ask new ELEVATE clients whether they have market research to share. I proposed asking newly booked couples about their decision, with the answers becoming another source to consider in ad copy and landing pages.\n\nThat was an idea to explore. What interests me is the different information that question could give us. A review can describe the experience you delivered. An account of the booking decision can help us understand what mattered while the choice was still open.",
      "ending": "Both can give us something useful to consider when we communicate why your business is a good fit for a couple's wedding.\n\n-Whitney"
    },
    "stories": null
  },
